$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM tert-Butyl
(2S)-2-{[4-(4-chlorophenoxy)phenoxy]methyl}pyrrolidine-1-carboxylate (6).
Method A. From 4 and 4-chlorophenol (5), with chromatographic separation.
A mixture of intermediate 4, 4-chlorophenol (5, 11.30 g, 87.9 mmol), CuI
(1.39 g, 7.3 mmol), Cs2CO3 (38.17 g, 117.2 mmol) and N,N-dimethylglycine
(1.51 g, 14.6 mmol) in 1,4-dioxane (200 mL) was heated to reflux under
nitrogen, overnight, cooled to ambient, concentrated in vacuo and
partitioned between hexane (500 mL) and water (250 mL). The organic layer
was washed with 0.2 M NaOH (2 x 250 mL) and water (250 mL). Concentration
in vacuo gave 10.56 g crude that was chromatographically purified on 800
g silica gel using 9:1 hexane:acetone as eluent to afford the product as
an oil that solidified upon standing (8.42g, 71.1% yield at only ca. 89%
purity). The main impurity in the product fraction was 7 which was also
isolated chemically pure from preceding fractions, in significant amount
(0.99 g), and characterized by 1H NMR.2 Another significant impurity, 8,
could not be isolated with reasonable purity and it was identified in the
product fraction using LC-MS.3 tert-Butyl
(2S)-2-{[4-(4-chlorophenoxy)phenoxy]methyl}pyrrolidine-1-carboxylate (6),
yield 8.42g, 71.1% 1H NMR (500 MHz, CDCl3) δ 1.47 (s, 9 H), 1.80-2.10 (m,
4 H), 3.30-3.50 (m, 2 H), 3.73-3.95 (m, 1 H), 4.03-4.20 (m, 2 H), 6.86
(d, 2 H, J = 9.2 Hz), 6.88-6.97 (m, 4 H), 7.24 (d, 2 H, J = 9.2 Hz). 13C
NMR (500 MHz, CDCl3; 2 rotamers) 22.69 and 23.66, 27.88 and 28.60, 28.38,
46.44 and 46.80, 55.75 and 55.86, 68.26 and 68.76, 79.12 and 79.48,
115.57, 118.56, 120.64, 127.10, 129.34, 149.53 and 149.74, 154.24 and
154.50, 155.23 and 155.31, 157.06. IR 2973, 2931, 2872, 1695, 1504, 1486,
1392, 1237, 1162, 1090, 828. MS (ES+) m/z 404.5 M+ + 1, 304.0 (base peak)
M+ + 1 - COOtBu. Mp 75.7°C. Anal. Calcd for C22H26ClNO4: C, 65.42; H,
6.49; Cl, 8.78; N, 3.47. Found: C, 65.39; H, 6.49; Cl, 8.82; N, 3.52.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM key step, alternative reaction conditions gave lower yield, Ullmann
conditions, scalable, 89% purity
